al-auxiliary shifts: may/might collapse, shall/must recede, dare/need de-auxiliarize", "file_type": "concept", "source_file": "established/073_english_language_historical_intro_pt09.md", "source_location": null, "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null}, {"id": "established_073_english_language_historical_intro_pt09_singular_they_external_motivation", "label": "Singular 'they' promoted to acceptability by external social/ideological pressure", "file_type": "concept", "source_file": "established/073_english_language_historical_intro_pt09.md", "source_location": null, "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "A pre-existing, stigmatized colloquial grammatical form (singular they) already supplied the needed gender-neutral function; the rise of feminism/equal-opportunities norms created demand that connected to this pre-existing 'slot', rather than inventing either anew."}, {"id": "established_073_english_language_historical_intro_pt09_closing_prescriptivism_reflection", "label": "Closing reflection: prescriptivism as generational bias vs. scientific detachment", "file_type": "concept", "source_file": "established/073_english_language_historical_intro_pt09.md", "source_location": null, "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "We are trained in one form of the language and dismiss innovations as mistakes, the same dismissal we recognize as mere generational grumbling when directed at our own usage - so personal conservatism and scientific non-judgmental description are framed as a required dual stance, not a contradiction."}, {"id": "established_073_english_language_historical_intro_pt09_vocab_hassle_american_slang_colloquial", "label": "hassle/heist/hype/scam/zap - American slang actively spreading into British English", "file_type": "concept", "source_file": "established/073_english_language_historical_intro_pt09.md", "source_locatio